language ESSENCE' 1.0
$ csplib_019_magic_squares_and_sequences: an Essence' model from DCP Rosetta.
$
$ A magic sequence of length n is a sequence of integers x_0, ..., x_(n-1), each between 0 and n-1, such that for every i from 0 to n-1 the number i occurs exactly x_i times in the sequence. For example, 6, 2, 1, 0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 0 is a magic sequence, since 0 occurs 6 times in it, 1 occurs twice, and so on.
$
$ Save this file as model.eprime. Download `csplib_019_magic_squares_and_sequences.json` and `dcp_convert_essence.py` from the
$ problem's page:
$   https://dcp-bench.github.io/DCP-Rosetta/problems/csplib_019_magic_squares_and_sequences.html
$ Convert the instances once, then run the first one with Savile Row 1.11 (it needs Java); the
$ solution is written to `csplib_019_magic_squares_and_sequences_1.param.solution`:
$   python dcp_convert_essence.py csplib_019_magic_squares_and_sequences.json
$   savilerow model.eprime csplib_019_magic_squares_and_sequences_1.param -run-solver

$ n: length of the sequence
given n : int(1..)

$ x[j]: the value at position j of the sequence, indices 1-based (so x[j] is x_(j-1) of the statement)
find x : matrix indexed by [int(1..n)] of int(0..n-1)

branching on [x]

such that
$ for each number i from 0 to n-1, i occurs exactly x_i times in the sequence (the count of value i is x[i+1])
forAll i : int(0..n-1) . ((sum j : int(1..n) . toInt(x[j] = i)) = x[i+1])
